Yes. The loop still works, but the verification claim needs to be much narrower than “this WorkerKit config caused this win.”

The clean rule should be:

> **Moltwork verifies externally observable facts. Sellers make everything else as explicit claims.**

A wallet transaction is useful evidence, but by itself it proves only that **address A paid address B amount X at time T**. It does not prove the payment came from legitimate work, that the seller did the work, or that a particular kit caused the result. ERC-8004 explicitly separates identity/reputation/validation from payments for essentially this reason. ([Ethereum Improvement Proposals][1])

## The evidence ladder

I think Moltwork only needs four levels.

| Evidence                 | Moltwork can say                                     | Example                             |
| ------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------- |
| **Payment receipt**      | “Payment verified”                                   | 23 USDC arrived                     |
| **External outcome**     | “Job accepted/won and payout verified”               | NEAR job accepted + escrow released |
| **Artifact link**        | “This committed artifact was the submitted artifact” | submitted repo/zip hash matches     |
| **Execution provenance** | “This exact run produced that artifact”              | Moltwork-controlled/attested runner |

Notice the wording gets progressively stronger.

### Level 1 — verified payment

Very easy with crypto.

```text
wallet A
↓
23 USDC
↓
wallet B
```

Moltwork verifies chain data.

Show:

```text
✓ PAYMENT VERIFIED
23 USDC
Base
tx 0x...
```

**Do not show:**

```text
✓ EARNED $23 DOING ROBLOX
```

because that requires more evidence.

And self-payments matter. Someone can transfer $1,000 between their own wallets. So raw wallet volume should never become seller reputation automatically.

---

# Level 2 is the really useful one: external work receipt

NEAR is a great example because the API exposes the lifecycle itself:

```text
job
→ award
→ submission
→ accept
→ escrow release
```

Its API has endpoints for submitting deliverables and requester acceptance/release of escrow. ([NEAR Market][2])

Therefore Moltwork can say:

```text
✓ EXTERNAL SUCCESS VERIFIED

Market: NEAR Agent Market
Job: #abc
Worker: Agent X
Status: accepted
Payout: $37.00
Completed: Aug 28
```

That's extremely strong evidence.

Full API docs:

[https://market.near.ai/api-docs/](https://market.near.ai/api-docs/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

For every Oracle marketplace, one of the things we should determine is:

```text
Can we independently retrieve:

job?
worker?
submission?
outcome?
payment?
```

Then give it an evidence capability:

```text
NEAR              █████ 5/5
Bittensor         ████░
Virtuals          ████░
random web gig    █░░░░
```

This becomes useful Oracle metadata anyway.

---

# Level 3 — prove the thing being sold was involved

This is where it gets trickier.

Suppose seller says:

> “This research process made me $37.”

We should **not** try to prove causality.

Instead, seller can commit the asset before the work:

```text
Research Kit
hash = ABC
timestamp = 10:00

        ↓

Worker does job

        ↓

submission
hash = XYZ

        ↓

NEAR accepts

        ↓

$37
```

If the platform lets us associate the submitted artifact with the committed artifact directly, great.

For something such as code:

```text
seller commits git commit:
abcd1234

submission references:
github.com/.../commit/abcd1234
```

Then we can legitimately say:

> **This exact artifact was used in a verified successful submission.**

GitHub already supports artifact attestations that bind software artifacts to where/how they were built, and those attestations can be independently verified. ([GitHub Docs][3])

Full docs:

[https://docs.github.com/en/actions/how-tos/secure-your-work/use-artifact-attestations/use-artifact-attestations](https://docs.github.com/en/actions/how-tos/secure-your-work/use-artifact-attestations/use-artifact-attestations?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

But a **starter pack or process** isn't usually identical to the submitted artifact.

So the strongest honest wording may simply be:

```text
✓ $37 SUCCESS VERIFIED

○ Seller states this kit was used
  during that successful run.
```

And that's okay.

---

# Level 4 — proving WorkerKit actually executed it

This is the part I would **not build for V1**.

Because if WorkerKit runs entirely on the seller's computer, then:

```text
run.json
config.json
trace.json
```

are ultimately controlled by the seller.

They can fabricate them.

Hashing a fake trace merely proves:

> This fake trace existed at timestamp T.

Sigstore/Rekor is excellent for proving that an identified signer signed a particular digest at a particular time; Rekor provides an append-only transparency log. It does **not** independently establish that everything asserted inside an arbitrary signed document is true. ([Sigstore][4])

Full references:

[https://docs.sigstore.dev/](https://docs.sigstore.dev/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

[https://docs.sigstore.dev/logging/overview/](https://docs.sigstore.dev/logging/overview/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

So don't pretend a signed WorkerKit log is proof.

Call it:

```text
◐ WORKERKIT ATTESTED
```

not:

```text
✓ MOLTWORK VERIFIED
```

